<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_insert.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-insert">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-insert">
  <primary>INSERT</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>INSERT</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>INSERT</refname>
  <refpurpose>create the vertices and edges a pattern describes</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
INSERT [ <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = ] <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> [, [ <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = ] <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> ] ...
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-insert-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>INSERT</command> and <xref linkend="cypher-create"/> are the same clause under two
   names. The parser builds an identical tree for both, so they accept the same patterns, raise
   the same errors and produce the same plan.
  </para>

  <para>
   Every element in the pattern is created, except an element already bound to a variable in
   scope. Such an element is reused as an endpoint and is not duplicated. An
   <command>INSERT</command> runs once per input row, and with no clause before it, it runs
   once.
  </para>

  <para>
   The variables the pattern binds are visible to the clauses that follow, and to later
   patterns in the same <command>INSERT</command>. Every edge needs exactly one label and a
   direction. Labels have to exist already, unless <varname>enable_graph_ddl</varname> is on,
   in which case they are created implicitly.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-insert-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Optional name bound to the whole path the pattern created. Returning it yields a
      <type>graphpath</type> value listing the new vertices and edges in order.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A vertex pattern or a path pattern, as described in <xref linkend="cypher-path"/>. A
      property map may be written inline, as in <literal>{title: 'Tenet'}</literal>, or
      supplied whole with <literal>=</literal> from an expression, including a SQL subquery.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-insert-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Add one film:
<programlisting>
INSERT (:movie {title: 'Tenet', year: 2020, genre: 'Action'});

UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The same statement written with <command>CREATE</command> creates the same vertex, and both
   plan identically:
<programlisting>
EXPLAIN (COSTS OFF) INSERT (:movie {title: 'Tenet'});

  QUERY PLAN
--------------
 Graph Create
   -&gt;  Result
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>INSERT</command> binds its variables like any other clause, so a following
   <command>RETURN</command> can report them:
<programlisting>
INSERT (p:person {name: 'John David Washington', born: 1984}) RETURN p.name AS person;

         person
-------------------------
 "John David Washington"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A variable bound by a preceding <command>MATCH</command> is an endpoint, not a new vertex,
   so only the edge is created:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'}), (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
INSERT (p)-[:directed]-&gt;(m)
RETURN count(*) AS edges;

 edges
-------
 1
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Patterns are separated by commas and may refer to variables that earlier patterns bound:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
INSERT (a:person {name: 'Robert Pattinson', born: 1986}),
       (b:person {name: 'Elizabeth Debicki', born: 1990}),
       (a)-[:acted_in {role: 'Neil'}]-&gt;(m),
       (b)-[:acted_in {role: 'Kat'}]-&gt;(m);

UPDATE 4
</programlisting>
   The tag counts the four elements the one clause created:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[r:acted_in]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
RETURN p.name AS actor, r.role AS role ORDER BY actor;

        actor        |  role
---------------------+--------
 "Elizabeth Debicki" | "Kat"
 "Robert Pattinson"  | "Neil"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The <literal>= <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable></literal> form
   supplies the whole map at once. Use it to load properties out of a table:
<programlisting>
INSERT (:movie = (SELECT jsonb_build_object('title', 'Dunkirk', 'year', 2017)));

UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Dunkirk'}) RETURN m.year AS year;

 year
------
 2017
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-insert-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   The clause reports the ordinary graph write command tag, <literal>UPDATE n</literal>, rather
   than a <command>CREATE</command> tag. Pair it with <xref linkend="cypher-finish"/> to write
   without projecting anything.
  </para>

  <para>
   Refusals name <command>CREATE</command> even when the query said
   <command>INSERT</command>, because both spellings build the same clause. The ones worth
   knowing are these.
  </para>

  <informaltable>
   <tgroup cols="2">
    <thead>
     <row>
      <entry>Pattern</entry>
      <entry>Error</entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
     <row>
      <entry><literal>INSERT ()-[]-&gt;()</literal></entry>
      <entry><literal>only one relationship type is allowed for CREATE</literal></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry><literal>INSERT ()-[:acted_in|directed]-&gt;()</literal></entry>
      <entry><literal>only one relationship type is allowed for CREATE</literal></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry><literal>INSERT ()-[:directed]-()</literal></entry>
      <entry><literal>only directed relationships are allowed in CREATE</literal></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry><literal>INSERT (:ag_vertex)</literal></entry>
      <entry><literal>specifying default label is not allowed</literal></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry><literal>INSERT ()-[:ag_edge]-&gt;()</literal></entry>
      <entry><literal>cannot create edge on default label</literal></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry><literal>INSERT (:directed)</literal></entry>
      <entry><literal>label "directed" is edge label</literal></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry><literal>INSERT ()-[:movie]-&gt;()</literal></entry>
      <entry><literal>label "movie" is vertex label</literal></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry><literal>INSERT (m)</literal>, where <literal>m</literal> is already bound</entry>
      <entry><literal>there must be at least one relationship</literal></entry>
     </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </informaltable>

  <para>
   <literal>insert</literal> became a reserved keyword in 2.18. It is still accepted unquoted
   wherever that leaves the grammar unambiguous, as a variable name in
   <literal>MATCH (insert:person)</literal> and as a label name in
   <literal>MATCH (n:insert)</literal>. It is not accepted as a
   <command>CREATE VLABEL</command> name, as an <command>ORDER BY</command> key naming a
   <command>RETURN</command> alias, or as a SQL table or column name. Write
   <literal>"insert"</literal> in those places, or rename.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-insert-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>INSERT</command> implements the GQL INSERT statement of ISO/IEC 39075:2024, section
   13.2, over the insert graph pattern of section 16.5. Inserting nodes and edges, several
   patterns per statement, reusing a bound element as an endpoint, inline property maps and a
   path variable over the inserted pattern are all supported. The typed graph checks of section
   18 are not, because they require a graph type. AgensGraph graphs are untyped, so an insert is
   validated against the labels that exist rather than against a declared node type or edge
   type.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher spells this clause <command>CREATE</command>, which AgensGraph accepts as a
   synonym. The <command>INSERT</command> name comes from GQL and from SQL, where it does the
   same job on a table.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

